<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1168 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Würzburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Franconian church state whose bishops styled themselves Dukes of Franconia and built one of Europe's grandest baroque palaces, the Würzburg Residenz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The diocese was founded in 742 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burchard-of-wurzburg">Burkard</a> as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Royal grants gave the bishops wide lands on the Main, and in 1168 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Emperor Frederick Barbarossa</a> confirmed their judicial and ducal rights across eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, the basis of their later title of Dukes of Franconia and of a territorial principality inside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> was elected by the noble cathedral chapter and governed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marienberg-fortress">Marienberg</a> fortress, later from the town below. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-echter-von-mespelbrunn">Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn</a> refounded the university in 1582, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliusspital">Juliusspital</a> and drove a forceful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. The 17th century saw some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> worst witch trials. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schonborn-family">Schönborn</a> bishops <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann">Balthasar Neumann</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence">Residenz</a>, decorated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-battista-tiepolo">Tiepolo's</a> ceiling frescoes, and vineyards along the Main supported a wealthy wine trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Secularised in 1802 and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, the territory became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-wurzburg">Grand Duchy of Würzburg</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Ferdinand of Tuscany</a> from 1805 to 1814 before returning to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>. The diocese survives, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence">Residenz</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg" width="320" height="282"><figcaption>Image: Johann Baptist Homann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince-bishopric_of_W%C3%BCrzburg_by_J.B._Homann.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1168 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Würzburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn, Johann Philipp Franz von Schönborn, Friedrich Karl von Schönborn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Würzburg, Duchy of Franconia (Würzburg)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1485&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4908 444 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5228 700L5229 694M5228 700L5269 749M5228 700L5228 700"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0M5228 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5228 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5228 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Würzburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1485&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-wurzburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
